<!--
    Bug: a CSS rule with a compound/chained pseudo-class selector (two pseudo-classes on one
    selector, e.g. `:down:disabled`) corrupts matching for an EARLIER, unrelated `:down`-only
    rule in the same stylesheet - not just failing to match itself.

    Expected: the button is selected (toggle=true, selected=true) and not disabled, so
    `.repro-button:down` should apply - red background.

    Actual: the button renders with its default (unstyled) background - neither red nor green -
    because the presence of the `.repro-button:down:disabled` rule below breaks the `:down`-only
    rule above it, even though the button never matches `:down:disabled` (it isn't disabled).

    Removing the `.repro-button:down:disabled` block (or replacing it with two separate,
    non-chained rules) makes `.repro-button:down` apply correctly.
-->
<vbox>
    <style>
        .repro-button:down {
            background-color: #ff0000;
        }

        .repro-button:down:disabled {
            background-color: #00ff00;
        }
    </style>

    <button id="btn" text="Selected (should be RED)" toggle="true" selected="true" styleName="repro-button" />
</vbox>
